38:04.500 --> 38:07.500
Do you know the difference between physical and functional?

38:13.860 --> 38:14.860
No.

38:16.200 --> 38:20.500
Okay, physical, it's targeting to a specific node.

38:21.500 --> 38:27.500
So, for example, I'm asking to the engine control module something.

38:27.500 --> 38:40.500
So, I have a direct request with a specific token ID addressing to the ECU 60,

38:40.500 --> 38:44.500
for example, in this case, while the tester is F1.

38:44.500 --> 38:58.500
I expect a response with this ID from the specific ECU to the tester.

38:58.500 --> 39:04.500
The functional is I'm asking to powertrain different information.

39:04.500 --> 39:10.500
You see that the KNIT is different, but even the ECUs are different,

39:10.500 --> 39:13.500
and this is part of the group, of a specific group.

39:13.500 --> 39:18.500
This is useful for OBD specification.

39:18.500 --> 39:26.500
Basically, when you need to check all the powertrain units,

39:26.500 --> 39:30.500
all the braking system and stuff like this, you will have functional addressing.

39:30.500 --> 39:38.500
While the physical addressing is specific to the single ECU,

39:38.500 --> 39:43.500
which can be the body, which can be the transmission, the engine, the DSP,

39:43.500 --> 39:48.500
the, I don't know, the seat modules, and stuff like this.

39:48.500 --> 39:50.500
